18:22:01,933 root DEBUG {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You will be given a SQL query and some strategies to rewrite the given SQL query. Your task is to summarize the provided query rewrite strategies into one paragraph.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nSQL Query:\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(t.title) AS american_vhs_movie\nFROM company_type AS ct,\n     info_type AS it,\n     movie_companies AS mc,\n     movie_info AS mi,\n     title AS t\nWHERE ct.kind = \'production companies\'\n  AND mc.note LIKE \'%(VHS)%\'\n  AND mc.note LIKE \'%(USA)%\'\n  AND mc.note LIKE \'%(1994)%\'\n  AND mi.info IN (\'USA\',\n                  \'America\')\n  AND t.production_year > 2010\n  AND t.id = mi.movie_id\n  AND t.id = mc.movie_id\n  AND mc.movie_id = mi.movie_id\n  AND ct.id = mc.company_type_id\n  AND it.id = mi.info_type_id;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""The rewritten logical plan shows that the original query is rewritten with a single `LogicalFilter` condition that combines all the filter conditions. This means that the query rewrite rule applies the aggregate function and group by to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before projection, as specified in Case 1.\n\nIn summary, the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by rewriting the `SELECT` clause with an aggregate function (`MIN`) and a column (`t.title`). The rewritten logical plan shows that the original query is rewritten with a single `LogicalFilter` condition that combines all the filter conditions."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""According to Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, if a filter condition is placed on the result set of an INNER JOIN operation and the filter\'s expressions do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join but can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs, move these conditions into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side.\n\nIn this scenario, the original query has multiple joins and filters. The filter conditions that only reference columns from one side of the join (e.g., `ct.kind = \'production companies\'`, `mc.note LIKE \'%(VHS)%\'`, etc.) can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs. Therefore, these conditions are moved into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side.\n\nThe rewritten query has fewer joins and more filters applied at different levels. For example, the filter `ct.kind = \'production companies\'` is now applied in the ON clause of the first join, and the filter `mc.note LIKE \'%(VHS)%\'` is now applied in the WHERE clause of the subquery for the movie_companies table.\n\nBy applying Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, we can simplify the original query and improve its performance by reducing the size of datasets before the join occurs."""'}], 'response': "Here's a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using two different strategies. The first strategy involves rewriting the logical plan to combine all filter conditions into a single `LogicalFilter` condition, applying an aggregate function and group by to the data source before projection. This is achieved by moving the filter conditions from the WHERE clause to the SELECT clause with an aggregate function like MIN. The second strategy applies Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, which involves moving filter conditions that only reference columns from one side of a join into the ON clause or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side. This simplifies the original query by reducing the size of datasets before the join occurs and can improve performance.", 'time': 2.2396464347839355}
